# pwebcam receiver config — copy to mac/.env and edit
#   cp mac/.env.example mac/.env
# Changes take effect after: bash mac/restart.sh  (or re-run python3 mac/receiver.py)
# Format: KEY=value, one per line. Lines starting with # are comments.

# ── Transport ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# How the receiver reaches the phone.
#
#   usb   ADB-forwarded USB only. Lowest latency (~55 ms), and the receiver
#         auto-starts the phone app via adb. This is the default.
#   wifi  LAN only. Finds the phone by mDNS (service _pwebcam._tcp), no adb needed.
#   auto  Per connection attempt: USB if an ADB device is present, otherwise Wi-Fi.
#         Unplug the cable and the next (re)connect falls back to Wi-Fi.
#
# Wi-Fi notes:
#   - The phone app must already be running (adb can't start it). It starts on
#     boot by default (Android 10 and older; newer Android may block it) or open it by hand.
#   - Phone and Mac must be on the same subnet, and the network must allow mDNS
#     (multicast). Guest/corporate Wi-Fi often doesn't — use PHONE_HOST below.
#   - The stream is plain MJPEG: unencrypted and unauthenticated. Use trusted networks only.
#   - Expect higher, jumpier latency than USB.
#
# Example: TRANSPORT=auto
#TRANSPORT=usb

# Fixed phone address for Wi-Fi/auto mode; skips mDNS discovery entirely.
# Use when multicast is blocked, or discovery picks the wrong device.
# Give the phone a DHCP reservation so the address doesn't change.
# Example: PHONE_HOST=192.168.1.50
#PHONE_HOST=

# ── ADB device targeting ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Only needed when more than one ADB device is connected (USB phone + TCP TV box,
# etc.); otherwise plain `adb` fails with "more than one device/emulator".
# Applies to USB (and the USB half of auto). Wi-Fi discovery ignores it.
# Use ONE of the options below, or leave both unset for single-device setups.

# Option A — pin to a specific serial (run `adb devices` to find yours).
# Most explicit; takes priority over Option B.
# Example: DEVICE=R5CR90XXXXXXX
#DEVICE=

# Option B — target by connection type (works only if exactly one device of that type):
#   usb     any single USB device     (adb -d)
#   tcpip   any single TCP/IP device  (adb -e)
#CONNECTION=usb

# ── Camera API ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Selects which phone port the receiver connects to; both servers run on the phone.
#   false  Camera1 API, port 8080. Software JPEG, most compatible. Recommended
#          for LIMITED Camera2 HALs (e.g. Kirin 659 / Huawei FIG-LX1), which
#          throttle JPEG output below 30 fps.
#   true   Camera2 API, port 8081. Hardware or software JPEG (toggle in the app);
#          best on devices with FULL Camera2 support (e.g. Pixel).
# Unset or blank = true.
#PREFER_CAMERA2=false
